var e=[{id:`en-stories-01`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`The train left the station just as I reached the platform. I stood there with my suitcase, out of breath, and watched it go. Then an old man beside me laughed and said, "Good. Now you have time for coffee."`},{id:`en-stories-02`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`My grandmother never used a recipe. She measured flour with her hands and knew the bread was ready by its smell. I have her oven now, but I still can't make it taste the same.`},{id:`en-stories-03`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`We got lost on the second day of the trip. The map said to turn left, the road said otherwise. In the end we found a tiny village with one bakery, and it sold the best apple cake I have ever eaten.`},{id:`en-stories-04`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`easy`,text:`The dog waited by the door every evening at six. He could not read a clock, but he was never wrong.`},{id:`en-stories-05`,category:`stories`,length:`long`,difficulty:`hard`,text:`It rained on the morning of the market, so only a few sellers came. A woman with a blue umbrella sold me a jar of honey and told me its whole story: which hills, which flowers, which summer. I paid twice the price and did not mind at all.`},{id:`en-stories-06`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`On my first day in the new city I knew nobody. I sat in a small cafe and ordered soup. The owner brought two spoons, sat down across from me, and asked where I was from.`},{id:`en-stories-07`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`easy`,text:`The lake was so still that morning that the mountains seemed to stand on their heads. Nobody spoke. Even the children were quiet.`},{id:`en-stories-08`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`My brother taught me to ride a bike by lying to me. "I'm holding on," he kept saying, long after he had let go. I was halfway down the street before I looked back.`},{id:`en-stories-09`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`easy`,text:`Every Sunday my father made pancakes. He always burned the first one and always said it was for the dog.`},{id:`en-stories-10`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`The old bookshop smelled of dust and coffee. The owner knew every book on every shelf, and he never let you leave with just one.`},{id:`en-stories-11`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`We arrived at the beach just before sunset. The water was cold, the sand was warm, and for a moment nobody thought about tomorrow.`},{id:`en-stories-12`,category:`stories`,length:`long`,difficulty:`hard`,text:`When I was seven, I planted an apple seed behind our house. My mother told me it would never grow. Thirty years later, my own children climb that tree every autumn, and my mother brings a basket and pretends she always believed in it.`},{id:`en-stories-13`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`easy`,text:`It started snowing at midnight. By morning the whole town was quiet and white, and school was closed.`},{id:`en-stories-14`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`The waiter in Lisbon did not speak English and I did not speak Portuguese. We pointed, we laughed, and somehow I ended up with the best fish of my life.`},{id:`en-stories-15`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`My first car was older than I was. The radio only played one station, and the left door only opened from the inside. I loved it.`},{id:`en-stories-16`,category:`stories`,length:`long`,difficulty:`hard`,text:`There is a bench in the park where an old couple sits every afternoon. They rarely talk. He reads the newspaper, she feeds the birds, and at exactly four o'clock they stand up, hold hands and walk home. I have watched them for years and I still do not know their names.`},{id:`en-stories-17`,category:`stories`,length:`short`,difficulty:`easy`,text:`The cat knocked the glass off the table, looked me in the eye, and walked away slowly.`},{id:`en-stories-18`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`In the mountains, the bus stopped in the middle of nowhere. The driver got out, picked some wild strawberries, shared them with everyone, and drove on.`},{id:`en-stories-19`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`My grandfather fixed watches for fifty years. He used to say that time is the only thing you can never repair, so you had better spend it well.`},{id:`en-stories-20`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`We missed the last ferry and had to stay on the island. The only hotel had one free room, no hot water and the most beautiful view I have ever seen.`},{id:`en-stories-21`,category:`stories`,length:`long`,difficulty:`hard`,text:`The night before my wedding I could not sleep. I went down to the kitchen and found my father there, making tea. We sat together until two in the morning and talked about everything except the wedding. It is still my favorite memory of him.`},{id:`en-stories-22`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`easy`,text:`She opened the letter, read it twice, and then started to laugh so hard that she had to sit down.`},{id:`en-stories-23`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`The market in Bangkok was loud, hot and crowded. I bought mango with sticky rice from a woman who smiled at my terrible accent and gave me an extra piece.`},{id:`en-stories-24`,category:`stories`,length:`medium`,difficulty:`medium`,text:`When the power went out, we lit candles and played cards. My little sister won every game, and we still don't know how.`}];export{e as default};